Remove entries from an archive (zip, 7z, tar, cpio and ar, and tar and cpio under any
codec). A folder takes
everything under it. A new archive is written beside the old one and swapped in, so a failed
or cancelled removal leaves it as it was; the entries that stay are copied without decoding,
except the rest of a 7z solid block that loses one, which is encoded again. Fails with
`NotFound` when nothing matches.

Usage: beezip remove [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE> [ENTRIES]...

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

  [ENTRIES]...
          Entries to remove (a folder takes everything under it)

Options:
      --glob <PATTERN>
          Also remove entries matching these patterns. Without `/` a pattern matches a name at any
          depth (`*.tmp`); with `/` the whole path (`build/**/*.o`)

      --exclude <PATTERN>
          Keep entries matching these patterns, even if `entries` or `glob` take them

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password, if entry names need one (a 7z with encrypted names), or a 7z block that is
          encoded again does

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How names stored as bytes are read, so `entries` match them as listed [default: from
          config, `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

      --dry-run[=<DRY_RUN>]
          Show what would be removed without writing anything
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
